	(text_box "Draft17.asc\nop amp: OPA356\nswitch: can use 1pC charge injection prob, is just loop gain\nTS5A1066DBVR"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 -208.2653 0)
		(size 43.18 20.3053)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "84ad49e4-1889-4dad-b849-b242007de51d")
	)
	(text_box "agoston agoston's patent, 1986\n\nblow by: capacitive coupling from the schottky's capacitance causing a fluctuation of the input signal.\n\nautomatic compensation: \nbasically, at sample node you get a sampled signal (const) + live input, scaled (time varying).\n\ndac adjusts varactor 70 such that the negative input of amp 86 receives a varying component that is of the same scale as the blow-by signal.\n\nso it cancels out.\n\nADC only activates once, when you turn on the calibrator signal generator\n\n\n\n\n\n=================================================\n\nThe positive feedback loop from amp 86 to integrator 50 back to the bridge common mode voltage is real and must be thought about.\n\nWith the original schematic there is a conflict. The thevenin resistance that C203 and 204 see is essentially R62, assuming R201 and R205 are both relatively small compared to it.\nTo prevent sample to sample positive feedback runaway, the input node for amplifier 86 must be discharged back to zero just before the sample strobe. This means that the RC constant of R62 and C203 must be at least a few time smaller than 1/Fsample. \nThe issue is that when the memory gate is activated, the integrator 50 will immediately start ramping its output, and if R62 is small, node Sa will immediately start ramping as well and the whole chain of amplifiers from 86 to 50 will start running away.\n\nThis causes kind of a conflict since you can't satisfy both conditions at once.\n\n\n \n\n\nThe way to remove it is as follows: (example with a + step input)\nfirst cycle happens, everything is at zero so all is gud\nAs integrator output node rises, node between R60, 62 voltage rises with RC exponential based on diode and other parasitic capacitances.\nNode Sa also rises, with RC curve of the diode and tline 14. \n\nThe RC constant of C203 + C204 and R205 must be fast enough to quickly discharge the common mode rise caused by the signal coming from R62/R66.\n\nThis makes the first constraint:\nRC(C203+C204, R205) << RC(R62, C14)\n\nAnd the nodes Sa and Sb must fully return to their original (steady state) voltages before the next sample strobe pulse happens. This implies the next constraint:\nRC(R62, C14) << 1/Fsample\n\n============================================\n\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 -181.61 0)
		(size 43.18 177.7418)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "a3aa943e-3cd8-418e-92b0-635c5354995d")
	)
	(text_box "if you use MACOM flip chip diodes, use car defroster epoxy to glue them, do not solder.\n\nLTC6268, the 500 MHz one is unity gain stable\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
